<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Dream Jobs – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Dream Jobs</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Are the Jobs of the Future?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose a detailed personal narrative. Interactive: Write collaborative stories or articles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose a detailed essay explaining why they chose a dream job, developing ideas across an introduction, body paragraphs, and a conclusion (e.g., "I chose engineering because I enjoy solving problems. I also like working on projects that require creativity. In the future, I hope to contribute to sustainable building projects.").
            Can write a story with peers about a future job (e.g., "In the future, I will be working in technology.").
            Can collaboratively write a description of skills needed for future careers (e.g., "New jobs will require creativity and problem-solving.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of futuristic jobs (e.g., AI ethicist, drone engineer, virtual reality designer) on the board or projector. Students, in pairs, brainstorm and write down 2-3 sentences about what these jobs *will involve* and what *skills they will require*. They share their written sentences briefly with another pair, using future simple for predictions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes an example sentence on the board using target vocabulary and grammar: "A drone engineer <b>will develop</b> new technologies. This job <b>will require</b> innovative problem-solving <b>skills</b> and strong adaptability." The teacher highlights the future simple verb "will develop" and "will require", and points out vocabulary like "technology," "innovation," "skills," and "adaptability." The teacher also models the stress in multisyllabic words like "technology" and "adaptability" as they write.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and sentence structure:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a job 'will require' a skill, does that mean you *need* that skill or you *might* need it?" (Expected answer: "You need it.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's the difference between 'predict' and 'demand' in the context of jobs?" (Expected answer: "Predict is guessing what will happen; demand is what is needed or wanted.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Dream Jobs"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short blog post (approx. 150-180 words) titled "Unlocking Tomorrow's Workforce: Your Dream Job in the Future," describing 2-3 emerging jobs (e.g., sustainable energy consultant, virtual reality architect) and the skills they will require, using future simple, future continuous, and modals for possibility. The text includes vocabulary such as <i>advancement, innovation, automation, remote work, adaptability, evolve, demand, acquire, career, technology, employment.</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose a detailed personal narrative.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the blog post silently and then complete the following writing tasks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question (written):</b> Write one sentence summarizing the main idea of the blog post.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (written):</b> Match the emerging job with the primary skill it will demand, based on the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (written):</b> Write 'T' or 'F' next to statements about future job predictions from the text, justifying false statements with a corrected sentence.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using Future simple (predictions), Future continuous (long-term goals), and Modals for possibilities (may, might, could, must). Target vocabulary: <i>jobs, skills, future, demand, advancement, automation, remote work, career, prediction, technology, opportunities, industries, AI, fields, employment, innovation, workforce, adaptability, education.</i> Verbs: <i>disappear, evolve, work, adapt, acquire, require, advance, change, predict, automate, replace, develop, demand, learn, prepare, innovate, foresee, anticipate, grow.</i> Adjectives: <i>future, relevant, technological, automated, remote, in-demand, adaptable, advanced, evolving, innovative.</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete sentences. They must choose appropriate vocabulary and grammar to complete each sentence, focusing on future predictions and requirements.
                        <br>Example: "By 2040, many traditional <b>jobs will have</b> ____ (disappear / evolve) due to <b>automation</b>."
                        <br>Example: "In five years, I <b>will be working</b> ____ (verb in future continuous) on innovative ____ (noun: projects/technology) that <b>may revolutionize</b> ____ (noun: industries/employment)."
                        <br>Example: "Future <b>careers will demand</b> high levels of ____ (adjective: adaptable/innovative) <b>skills</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Paragraph Construction):</b> Students are given a list of 5-7 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>AI, remote work, adaptability, evolve, innovation, predict</i>) and 3 sentence starters related to future jobs. In small groups (3-4 students), they collaboratively write a short paragraph (3-5 sentences) using all the provided words and sentence starters, ensuring correct use of future tenses and modals. The focus is on integrating the vocabulary and grammar into a coherent piece of writing.
                        <br>Example starters: "The rise of <b>AI will significantly change</b>..." "Many professionals <b>will be embracing remote work</b>..." "To succeed, we <b>must develop adaptability</b>..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Feedback & Correction:</b> Groups exchange their written paragraphs. Each group reviews another group's paragraph, identifying any grammatical errors (future tenses, modals), vocabulary misuse, or spelling mistakes. They provide written feedback using green pens (or similar) on a separate sheet, pointing out specific areas for improvement, and suggesting corrections. This encourages attention to detail and accuracy in writing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Dream Jobs"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Descriptive Essay.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "My Future Dream Job: A Glimpse into Tomorrow"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs to compose a detailed essay (approximately 150-200 words) describing their individual or shared "dream job" in the future. The essay must include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Introduction:</b> Briefly introduce the chosen dream job and its significance in the future.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Body Paragraph 1 (What will it be like?):</b> Describe the daily tasks and environment of this job, making predictions using the future simple (e.g., "I <b>will be working</b> in a highly automated lab," "My responsibilities <b>will involve developing</b> AI solutions.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Body Paragraph 2 (Skills & Evolution):</b> Explain the specific <b>skills</b> (e.g., <b>adaptability, innovation, technological</b> expertise) that will be <b>required</b> for this role and how the job might <b>evolve</b> over time. Use modals for possibilities (e.g., "This role <b>may demand</b> strong communication," "Our understanding of <b>AI will advance</b>, so the job <b>might change</b>.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Conclusion:</b> Summarize why this dream job is important and what impact it <b>will have</b> on society or their personal goals. Students should remember to structure their writing clearly and logically, incorporating at least 8-10 target vocabulary words from the list provided (e.g., <i>career, demand, advancement, technology, employment, workforce, opportunities, acquire</i>). During the writing process, they should also consider appropriate tone and language choices for a descriptive essay, practicing the sociolinguistic competence of presenting insights about future job markets.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during collaborative writing and peer assessment using a checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each pair submits their collaboratively written "My Future Dream Job" essay.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer & Teacher):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Cohesion:</b> Is the essay well-organized with a clear introduction, body, and conclusion? Are ideas logically connected?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Correct and varied use of future simple, future continuous, and modals for possibility.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Effective and appropriate use of target vocabulary related to jobs, skills, and the future (at least 8 distinct words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Does the essay fully describe the dream job, its requirements, and its evolution?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Evidence of shared writing effort.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (written):</b> Students individually reflect on their learning and participation:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing your essay, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new vocabulary word or grammar structure will you try to use more in your future writing?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflecting on the learning process</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> In their notebooks, students answer:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has this writing activity helped you think more clearly about your own career goals and the skills you might need to acquire?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific thing you learned about writing descriptions of future events or possibilities?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to promote writing fluency and accuracy? (e.g., "The collaborative essay format highly engaged students and encouraged peer support in structuring their ideas and selecting appropriate vocabulary.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to further improve their descriptive writing skills, especially regarding complex future scenarios? (e.g., "Some students struggled with varying sentence structures; next time, I will provide more sentence frames for complex sentences or specific conjunctions to connect ideas more smoothly.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This detailed writing practice in describing future jobs and required skills will prepare students for Lesson 5, where they will mediate and integrate information about future job trends and present their findings, possibly through a summary or a persuasive argument about the importance of certain skills. The ideas generated in their essays can be the foundation for the 21st Century Skills Project.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>